Phase 9: Timestamp comments ⭐

This is the feature that makes VidTalk interesting.

Suppose the video is currently at:

02:35

User writes:

"This part is important."

We store:

timestamp = 155 seconds

Then the Android UI can display a marker:

Video timeline

0 ────────●────────────●────────── 10:00
          ↑
        2:35

Tap the marker:

seekTo(155000)

and the video jumps directly to that moment.

Phase 10: Replies

Comments become threaded:

Comment
 ├── Reply
 ├── Reply
 └── Reply

Database relationship:

comments.parent_comment_id
Phase 11: Video comments

Now the more advanced feature.

User records a short video response:

CameraX
   ↓
Record video
   ↓
Android
   ↓
FastAPI
   ↓
Cloudinary
   ↓
Video URL
   ↓
PostgreSQL

We'll impose the intended duration limit when we implement this feature.

Phase 12: Cloudinary

Instead of putting large video files inside PostgreSQL:

PostgreSQL
    ❌ video file

we'll use:

Cloudinary
    ↓
actual video

PostgreSQL
    ↓
video URL

This is an important real-world architecture concept.

Phase 13: Reactions

Add:

👍 Like
👎 Dislike

with the appropriate database design so one user cannot create conflicting duplicate reactions.

Phase 14: Room / local caching

We'll introduce:

Room

for local Android storage.

Architecture becomes:

             Android
                │
             ViewModel
                │
           Repository
           /         \
       Retrofit      Room
          │            │
      FastAPI       Local DB

We'll use it where local caching/offline behavior actually makes sense, rather than adding it just because it's in the tech stack.

